+# aiosendspin's resampler (stereo->mono for an Echo) stamps its output from
+# its own running clock and only re-anchors when the input timestamps drift
+# more than 20 ms from it. When a chunk comes in late, the stream timeline is
+# rebased forward by a few ms so audio stays at least the lead ahead; the
+# resampler ignores each such jump and keeps stamping on the old timeline, so
+# every frame after it is stamped EARLY. Jumps pile up to just under 20 ms:
+# measured on a Radar at 80 ms lead, a stream started by stop/start (parec
+# delivers a few late chunks while it starts) arrived with 70 ms of margin, a
+# reselect (which recreates the resampler) with 78. The audio also plays that
+# much earlier than the lead says, which matters for lip-sync.
+#
+# Follow the input timeline instead: move the output clock by the same jump.
+# The filter graph is kept, so there is no click beyond the gap the rebase
+# itself made. Replaces push_stream._resample_pcm_standalone (aiosendspin
+# 9.1.1), which push_stream calls through its module global.
